    'Matrix linear algebra in the Gilbert Strang 18.06 tradition: linear systems, Gaussian elimination, matrix algebra, LU, vector spaces, independence, basis and dimension, linear maps, nullspace and column space, orthogonality, projections and least squares, determinants, eigenvalues, and a first look at SVD. You will write small proofs and you will implement Gaussian elimination in Python — not as a numerical-analysis course, but so elimination is an algorithm you have seen fail. 3Blue1Brown is intuition, not a substitute for Strang.',
  whyLanguageOrBook:
    'Strang’s MIT 18.06 is the linear algebra course that computer scientists actually finish. The lectures plus Introduction to Linear Algebra (or the free OCW notes/assignments) are the spine. 3Blue1Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra is assigned as geometric intuition before or after the corresponding Strang lecture — never instead. Linear Algebra and Learning from Data (Strang) is the O’Reilly-adjacent extra if you already know you are heading toward ML; it is optional.',
