    'Probability as a language (Blitzstein Stat 110) plus the statistics a working programmer actually ships: estimation, confidence intervals, hypothesis tests, bootstrap, correlation and regression, and a light Bayesian pass. Simulation in Python is part of the course, not a side quest. The computational project is a system you simulate (hash collisions or queues) and a report with uncertainty stated in numbers, not vibes. This is not a proof-only measure-theory course and it is not a button-clicking stats lab.',
